# /etc/pam.d/sudo-fingerprint — installed by sudo-fprint-gate (sudo-fprint-gate.sh).
# Edit the repository copy and re-run the installer.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
#
# Included by /etc/pam.d/sudo and /etc/pam.d/sudo-i, immediately before
# `@include common-auth`. The PASSWORD comes first and works at once. This
# pam_unix has no nullok, unlike common-auth's: an empty password never passes
# here, so this check accepts no password that common-auth's would refuse, and
# an account with an empty password is left to common-auth. An empty Enter (or
# a wrong password) falls through to the gate, which offers the fingerprint only
# for sudo, a local account, a real terminal and the lid open. When the finger
# fails, or is not offered, common-auth follows: its pam_unix re-checks the SAME
# password (it reuses the cached one), fails it, and its pam_deny ends this
# attempt. The new prompt comes from sudo trying again, so each detour costs one
# of sudo's tries. A correct password here ENDS the auth chain, so
# sudo-fprint-gate.sh refuses to install in front of one that holds anything
# this early check would skip past (pam_faillock, say): common-auth and every
# file sudo or sudo-i brings in after this include.
#
# The gate FAILS CLOSED. Its exit 0 ("offer") is ignored, so pam_fprintd runs
# next. ANY other outcome takes default=1 and jumps over pam_fprintd: its exit 1
# ("skip"), and equally a gate that is missing, not executable or crashed. The
# gate never decides the outcome itself.
#
# The jump must land exactly one line past pam_fprintd. A jump three lines
# further lands on common-auth's `required pam_permit.so`, which authenticates
# with no credential at all. sudo-fprint-gate.sh checks every jump statically and
# runs this stanza through the real libpam with a wrong password before any
# write, and --check does both again on the installed copy.
#
# quiet_log, because every skip is a non-zero exit that pam_exec would otherwise
# log as a failure; the gate logs its own reason. Why each line is shaped as it
# is: docs/guide.md.
auth  sufficient                    pam_unix.so
auth  [success=ignore default=1]    pam_exec.so quiet quiet_log /usr/local/libexec/fingerprint-should-offer
auth  sufficient                    pam_fprintd.so max-tries=2 timeout=10
